{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["Now he begins the Mishneh Torah: he says the Ten Commandments to them. He changed nothing and explained nothing in <b>I am YHWH</b> and <b>You shall have no other god</b>, for they heard them from the mouth of the Almighty. And so too in the third commandment, because He had already said (Exodus 20:7) <b>the name of YHWH your Elohim</b>, as I explained in the section <i>Kedoshim</i> (Leviticus 19:12). But he began to explain to them <b>Observe</b> in place of <b>Remember</b>, according to the plain sense. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Shavuot 20b), they were spoken in a single utterance, and he explained this to them, for they all knew <b>Remember</b>, and he informed them that <b>Observe</b> was spoken with it. And likewise by way of truth they were spoken in a single utterance, for the voice was in <b>Remember</b> and <b>Observe</b> because the Torah is an eternal covenant arranged in all and guarded, and He is the one who hears <b>Remember</b> and they <b>Observe</b>, and the enlightened one will know this from what we hinted there (Exodus 20:8). And he explained <b>as YHWH your Elohim commanded you</b> — before the giving of the Torah at Marah, where He established for them statute and ordinance; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 5:12). And if so, the commandment of honoring father and mother would also be at Marah, yet the Rav did not mention there anything except Sabbath, the red heifer, and civil laws. And what seems right in my eyes by way of truth regarding this, and in all of the Mishneh Torah, is that Moses speaks as from the mouth of the Almighty, as He said (Deuteronomy 11:13–14), <b>And it shall be, if you diligently hearken to My commandments which I command you this day, to love YHWH your Elohim and to serve Him with all your heart and with all your soul, then I will give the rain of your land in its season</b> — and Moses is not the one who gives rain upon the face of the earth, nor the one who gives grass in the field. Rather, the utterance in the Torah is in <b>Remember</b> and in the Mishneh Torah in <b>Observe</b>; therefore Moses says in every place in the Mishneh Torah <b>YHWH your Elohim</b>, whereas in the Torah he mentions the uniquely designated name alone, except in the Ten Commandments, as it says (Exodus 20:1), <b>And Elohim spoke</b>, etc. — and understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The phrase 'והוא השומע זכור והם שמור' is translated hyper-literally; the subject pronoun 'He' appears to refer to the divine voice, but the construction is cryptic and may carry a kabbalistic resonance left intentionally opaque."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:39.828693618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}